      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{62df7cd83e007e467295c4e1|full_name}: Oh sure you can, GET parameters could conceivably be used to send all sorts of nefarious data up to EvilSite.com.  If only there was a way to detect that and strip it out of a String...\n\nThat's why I proposed loosening it only for the \"trusted sites\" (and by that, I mean the sites you don't have to toggle Allow Untrusted URLs for, that's what I meant by \"the hosts you 'trust'\", with \"you\" being the VRChat company.)  Even if you embed data in a url, unless you're an inside evildoer at one of the trusted sites, then you're not going to get much out of \"https://gist.github.com/usernameIsOoops/usersSSNis123456789/usersLocationIsAt12345ChangeMyLuggageSecurityCode\"",
      "viewerReactions": null,
