  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "I was in the process of building a world dedicated to my favorite podcast, when I ran into the brick wall of being unable to dynamically construct VRCUrl objects.  This is a problem because my intention was to make it such that the world just pulls a json from a github gist to get the catalog of episodes (which means, I don't have to upload the world every time a new episode of the podcast is released.)  I got that working, then found out that I cannot ctor VRCUrls (thanks for making that upfront and clear, btw.  And before some whiteknight chirps up that it's documented somewhere, I poured over the docs and never saw it, so at best it's hidden away in tiny print squirrelled away in someplace the sun doesn't shine.  The only way I learned about it, after coding for 3 hours, is a warning message generated in the Console log.)\n\nSo, look, I get why you do that, you consider it a security risk, and somehow baking that into editor-constructed VRCUrl instances somehow mitigates that? (doubtful, if someone isn't vetting every world upload, besides that's the job of Allow Untrusted URLs.)\n\nI'd like to propose loosening that up a bit, and allowing dynamic URLs targeting the hosts you \"trust\" -- ie, YouTube, Discord, Github, etc.  It should be trivially easy to add a check for that in the VRCUrl ctor.  If you have to, make it a requirement that the world uploader must be a VRC+ subscriber (non-gifted) in order to enforce accountability.",
